{
  "abstract": "Paper 18's census of regular build orders gives septuple surfaces on which every rational point is a Diophantine septuple (cost C=0). Their least coordinate degree is 21, and small searches see only frames with a degree-2 coordinate. We add Gibbs's relation P6 as a step and closure support, with the exact discriminant 16 times the product of the ten pair values. With P6 off, our code reproduces paper 18's counts exactly (1511 surface and 258 threefold closure orders). With P6 there are 4298 surface orders, 2787 of which use P6, in 89 new hypergraph classes, 85 genuine on the line test. The least observed coordinate degree drops to 12. On the seven lightest classes all 21 values are squares at every tested F_p-point, and no hidden relation holds. Because what makes the DKMS frame searchable is an elliptic fibration, we then gather computational evidence on fibre genera. We reconstruct one coordinate section per direction mod p, on both root branches, certify its components absolutely irreducible over F_p, and bound genera by Riemann-Hurwitz. The DKMS control gives genus exactly 1. Every component for the seven lightest P6 classes has bound at least 13, and for paper 18's six lightest at least 131. On five twin quotients of paper 17's chain-closure surfaces, where a septuple costs one square, exact mod-1009 resultants give genus at least 33 on each invariant pencil's chosen fibre. These are mod-p statements about chosen fibres. We state precisely what a characteristic-0 transfer would need, and we do not claim it. No septuple is found or excluded.",
